# Gate review — the `(compile ...)` known-gap landing

Scope: `git diff origin/master fbfb82b6` — 4 files, +43/−13 (`yin/repl.cljc`, `cst.cljs`, `cst.cljd`, `python_antlr_test.cljc`). Read-only; no lanes re-run per the prompt.

## 1. The priming fix — CONFIRMED

- `compile-command-ast` (`src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:1751-1762`) rebinds `state` lexically and returns only the lowered AST. `handle-command` (`:1871-1873`) returns its own unchanged `state` to the loop; the rebound state never escapes. The new equality assertion (`python_antlr_test.cljc:552-553`) pins it.
- `:frontend-session` has exactly one production reader — the equality test `(= :primed session)` in `yang/python/antlr/repl.cljc:99-102` — and its only writer from lowering is `commit-frontend` (`repl.cljc:1063-1066`). Nothing calls methods on it. `:primed` is the frontend's own session vocabulary, not a foreign object.
- Only Python installs `:lower-session` (`yin/repl/frontends.cljc:73`); Clojure and PHP have no `:lower-session`, so `lower-parsed` ignores the fabricated marker for them. Blast radius is the Python preview only.
- Does `:primed` "lie" downstream? It rides on the AST as `::frontend {:session :primed}` metadata (`repl.cljc:1723`), but the preview is clean: `format-value` → `quote-symbols` → `render-map` rebuilds values and drops metadata (`repl.cljc:394-425`), and `encoder/program-batch` projects grammar slots only (`yin/vm.cljc:1114-1124`). No AST/rows/events section claims a primed session.
- Honest nuance: on a fresh session the preview omits the prelude that the *first real submission* would carry. That is exactly the fallback contract ("the submitted form, the prelude implicit"), and the new docstring states it.

## 2. The error-classifier change — CONFIRMED, no lost `:end`

- The only caller of `first-failure` is the completeness probe (`repl_probe.cljc:163,168`); `failure-kind` has no other caller and no other consumer depends on its index.
- `failure-kind` (`export.cljc:143-159`) returns `:end` iff the failing index > the last real token. The listener's `offending` is `LT(1)` in both changed runtimes: JS 4.13.2 constructs `new NoViableAltException(parser, input, input.get(startIndex), input.LT(1), …)`, Dart 4.13.2 does the same (`parser_atn_simulator.dart:2092-2099`) and reports via `e.offendingToken`. At a genuine end-of-input failure `LT(1)` is the EOF token (index `size-1`, always above `last-real`) or a trailing zero-width NEWLINE/DEDENT (excluded from `last-real` by `start <= stop`) → `:end`. The probe's parking contract is preserved.
- The removed branch could only produce an index **≥** `LT(1)`'s (`dec(buffer length)` is the furthest buffered token), so it could only turn `:early` into false `:end` — never the reverse. Dropping it cannot lose a genuine `:end`.
- Why the hosts differ: JVM `BufferedTokenStream.getText(Interval)` does not fill to EOF; JS/Dart `getText`/`getTextRange` call `fill()` before the listener fires. The branch was fatal only on JS/Dart. `tokens` remains used on both hosts for parser construction and real-token enumeration — no dead binding.
- Index check for the fix: `(compile "1 + 2")\n` → offending STRING at 2, `last-real` = 4 (NEWLINE) → `:early` → complete → command runs; pre-fix `dec(length)` = 5 (EOF) → `:end` → parked → `""`. Same for `(compile 1)`, which is why the error-path test also covers the fix.

